\section{Estimands and conventions}\label{sec:S1}

We compared arrays of optically pumped magnetometers (OPMs) with the Neuromag system, a magnetoencephalography (MEG) system of superconducting quantum interference devices (SQUIDs). Unless stated otherwise, the comparator is all << F.g2_squid_channels >> channels of the Neuromag system. The primary metric is the known-topography detectability
\begin{equation}
d = \sqrt{s^{\mathsf{T}} C^{-1} s},
\label{eq:S-detectability}
\end{equation}
where $s$ is the field pattern of a source across an array's channels and $C$ is that array's noise covariance; $d$ is the output signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) of the optimal matched filter for a source of known topography.

We computed $d$ with the true (oracle) covariance and rank-aware whitening for every target and every array, and compared arrays target by target. The adult comparison reports the median over targets of the paired ratio $d_{\mathrm{OPM}}/d_{\mathrm{Neuromag}}$, not the ratio of the two systems' median detectabilities. The same quantity in decibels is $D = 20\log_{10}(d_{\mathrm{OPM}}/d_{\mathrm{Neuromag}})$, positive when the OPM array is ahead; $D$ does not depend on the dipole moment.

The adult analyses report the unweighted median over all << F.g2_n_targets >> targets, the << F.g2_medialwall_n >> on the medial wall included. The smaller heads use an area-weighted median over cortical targets with the medial wall excluded, because their cortices differ in size and their targets in density. Both medians summarize the same metric. At the adult's measured head position, the dense array's ratio is << F.g3b_link_g2_ratio_all_targets_3dp >> unweighted over all targets, << F.g3b_link_g2_ratio_cortical_3dp >> without the medial wall and << F.wr_adult_dense_ib_ratio_area_weighted_cortical_3dp >> area-weighted without it. Its $D$ is << F.wr_adult_dense_ib_db >>~dB under the adult convention and << F.g3b_link_g2_db >>~dB under the pediatric one. A 1\,\% change of the ratio is << F.wr_db_per_percent >>~dB, and 1~dB is a ratio of << F.wr_ratio_per_db >>. Table~\ref{tab:S1} gives the adult's primary comparison in both forms, and Table~\ref{tab:S2} lists every estimand of the study, its definition, its interval and what that interval covers.

