Every $\Delta$ is taken against the adult placed by the same rule, because the adult's own $D$ depends on the placement. It is << F.g3b_adult_centred_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >>~dB at its measured head position, << F.g3b_adult_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >>~dB at top contact and << F.g3b_adult_xcentred_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >>~dB laterally centered and raised to contact. It is << F.g3b_adult_top18mm_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >>~dB at true contact, << F.g3b_adult_cf_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >> and << F.g3b_adult_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >>~dB in its own helmets scaled with the head (centered and laterally centered; their scale factor is << F.g3b_adult_cfx_k >>), and << F.rev_cgap_adult_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >>~dB in the helmet fitted at its own gap. Where a difference of two pooled medians is used instead of $\Delta$, the text says so. $\Delta$ is reported descriptively, without a test or a margin.

Peak-channel SNR lets magnetometers and gradiometers compete on equal terms, because each channel is divided by its own noise. It is the closest analogue of the sensor-level SNRs of the clinical studies, with one difference: a clinical baseline standard deviation holds physiological noise as well as sensor and room noise, whereas the model's holds only what the noise model contains. Mean-power SNR is the channel-averaged SNR of \citet{goldenholz2009}, also used by \citet{matsubara2026}.

The plug-in filter is built from a Ledoit--Wolf covariance estimate \citep{ledoit2004} and evaluated under the true covariance, so it cannot exceed the oracle value. The estimate used << F.g2_plugin10s_samples >> or << F.g2_plugin60s_samples >> independent samples, the time-bandwidth equivalents of << F.g2_cov_seconds >>~s of data in this band, drawn for all arrays from one common noise realization. The estimate from << F.g2_plugin60s_samples >> samples is the declared << F.wr_cov_seconds_long >>-s scenario; its samples are independent, which does not establish the effective sample size of a measured colored-noise baseline. The estimate from << F.g2_plugin10s_samples >> samples shows how a short baseline penalizes the array with more channels.

Neighboring cortical targets are correlated, so no interval resamples targets as if they were independent. The one exception is the adult's frequency-band comparison, which resamples targets and therefore has narrower intervals than the primary comparison. All intervals are percentile intervals. In the spike study the location is the statistical unit: S50 ratios carry location-bootstrap intervals, and detection differences are tested with the sign-flip test on per-location differences in detection counts, reported exactly over all sign patterns (Section~\ref{sec:S7}). Table~\ref{tab:S16} gives the resample count of every interval family.
